Hello 3Dprinting.com team,

a targeted suggestion for your software overview rather than a general pitch.

Solidon3D sits in a category that is hard to place: it is not a CAD system and
not a mesh editor. It takes downloaded models (STL, 3MF, OBJ, STEP and others)
and makes them editable at feature level without CAD knowledge — move a hole,
change a countersink, add a thread that actually fits.

With version 0.5.0 one line in any comparison table changes: the result is no
longer a mesh. Primitives are built with real faces and edges; holes, slots,
countersinks, bosses, beads, grooves and threads stay exact through moving,
duplicating, rotating and removing; and STEP goes in and comes back out
cleanly. So a part edited in Solidon can be carried into a CAD system.

The other entries that may be relevant for a guide:

* Licensing: one-time purchase in three tiers, no subscription. After a single
  activation the application runs fully offline — no account, no telemetry, no
  server requirement. A machine without a network connection can be activated
  via a file and a second device.
* Platforms: Windows, macOS and Linux, with native Linux packages.
* Languages: interface and complete manual in six languages.
* AI: present, optional, and able to run locally on the user's own GPU. No
  feature depends on it except the chat itself.
* Printing side: printer profiles including resin devices with pixel size and
  minimum wall, printability checks, orientation search, plate layout and
  handover to common slicers.

70 user-visible changes in this release; 896 since the first 0.1 demo.

If a screenshot or a review licence would help to place the entry, I will
provide either.

Solidon3D is a desktop application for Windows, macOS and Linux. One-time
purchase, no subscription, no account: after a single activation it runs fully
